.TH YEW-FL 1 "2026-10-12" "yew" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yew-fl \- run the Fletch language used by yew
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yew fl
.br
.B yew fl
.I FILE
.br
.B yew fl \-e
.I EXPR
.br
.B yew fl \-c
.I FILE
.br
.B yew fl \-\-dump-ast
.I FILE
.br
.B yew fl \-\-dump-bytecode
.I FILE
.br
.B yew fl \-\-list-natives
.PP
The options
.BR \-\-caps " and " \-\-origin
may precede any of these forms.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Fletch is yew's configuration, automation, plugin, and recorded-macro
language.
It is a small, deterministic language with editor handles, motion literals,
lexical closures, catchable errors, and transactional
.B edit
blocks.
.PP
With a terminal and no operand,
.B yew fl
starts an interactive prompt.
Without a terminal it reads a complete script from standard input.
Supplying
.I FILE
evaluates that file without taking terminal ownership.
Diagnostics go to standard error; output from
.B io.print
goes to standard output.
A script invoked through this command takes no arguments; use
.BR "yew \-\-batch" (1)
when a script needs editor buffers or an argument list.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " EXPR"
Evaluate one expression and print its value when it is not
.BR nil .
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE"
Parse and compile the file, but do not run it.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump-ast " FILE"
Print the parsed abstract syntax tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump-bytecode " FILE"
Disassemble every compiled function.
.TP
.B \-\-list-natives
Print every module native as
.IR module.name ,
one per line.
.TP
.BI \-\-caps " SPEC"
Limit the entry script's capabilities.
.I SPEC
is
.BR all ,
.BR none ,
or a comma-separated list drawn from
.BR fs.read ,
.BR fs.write ,
.BR shell ,
and
.BR net .
The default for the command-line origin is
.BR all .
.TP
.BI \-\-origin " KIND"
Run with origin
.BR cli ,
.BR config ,
.BR workspace ,
or
.BR plugin .
The default is
.BR cli .
Changing the origin can only reduce what the script may reach.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the Fletch command synopsis.
.SH THE REPL
The primary prompt is
.BR "fl> " ;
an incomplete expression uses the continuation prompt
.BR "..> " .
Enter compiles and runs a complete form.
An open bracket or trailing operator continues onto the next line.
Values other than
.B nil
are printed in deterministic Fletch notation.
Rendering is bounded; a value too deep or too large is elided rather than
allowing the prompt to grow without limit.
.PP
The prompt commands are:
.TP
.B :help [NAME]
List prompt commands, or show a builtin signature.
.TP
.B :quit
Leave the prompt.
.B :q
is an alias.
.TP
.BI :load " PATH"
Evaluate a file in the current prompt while retaining its globals.
.TP
.B :reload
Evaluate the most recent
.B :load
path again.
.TP
.B :globals
List user-bound globals in insertion order.
.TP
.BI :disasm " NAME"
Disassemble a function bound in the prompt.
.TP
.B :caps
Show the current origin and grants.
.PP
.B Ctrl-C
discards the input being edited.
.B Ctrl-D
on an empty line leaves the prompt.
.B Ctrl-L
clears the screen.
There is no stepping debugger or breakpoint facility in 1.0; traces,
.BR :disasm ,
.BR \-\-dump-bytecode ,
and
.B io.print
are the debugging surfaces.
.SH LANGUAGE
Fletch values are
.BR nil ,
booleans, wrapping signed 64-bit integers, doubles, immutable
grapheme-indexed strings, mutable lists, insertion-ordered maps, functions,
and editor handles for buffers, cursors, spans, windows, and regular
expressions.
Only
.B nil
and
.B false
are false; zero, empty strings, and empty lists are true.
.PP
Functions are lexically scoped and closures capture shared variables by
reference.
Maps iterate in insertion order and strings compare bytewise, making a run
independent of locale and hash-table order.
The terse motion layer uses values such as
.BR l ,
.BR w ,
and
.B b
for editor motions.
A top-level
.B edit {
block is one undo transaction: an escaping error rolls back the block and
then re-raises; nested
.B edit
blocks join the outer transaction.
.PP
Runtime errors are maps with
.B kind
and
.B msg
fields.
The closed 1.0 kind set is
.BR type ,
.BR arity ,
.BR name ,
.BR index ,
.BR key ,
.BR div ,
.BR capability ,
.BR io ,
.BR import ,
.BR motion ,
.BR user ,
.BR limit ,
and
.BR handle .
Compile diagnostics are not catchable; runtime errors are.
The complete grammar and semantics are in
.IR .docs/fletch-spec.md ,
and the introductory book is installed with yew's documentation.
.SH MODULES
The closed language-standard module set is
.BR str ,
.BR list ,
.BR map ,
.BR math ,
.BR fmt ,
.BR io ,
and
.BR re .
An editor-hosted VM additionally exposes editor modules including
.BR buf ,
.BR win ,
.BR cur ,
.BR span ,
.BR opt ,
and
.BR ed .
File imports are resolved beside the importer, then below the user Fletch
configuration directory, then below
.BR YEW_RUNTIME_DIR .
Modules are cached by real path; an import cycle is an error that names the
cycle.
.SH CAPABILITIES
Host I/O is divided into
.BR fs.read ,
.BR fs.write ,
.BR shell ,
and
.BR net .
The origin of the active frame supplies authority; calling through a more
privileged function does not borrow its authority.
The command-line origin receives all four by default because the user who
invoked it already has a shell.
Use
.B \-\-caps
to reduce that set.
A denial raises a catchable
.B capability
error and an uncaught denial exits 3.
.SH BATCH MODE
.B yew \-\-batch
uses the same compiler and VM but adds an editor host.
It loads configuration, opens file operands in order, installs the globals
.BR args ,
.BR script_path ,
.BR files ,
and
.BR batch ,
runs the script, and exits without initializing a tty, terminal grid, or
event loop.
.PP
Standard output carries only script output.
Standard error carries diagnostics, runtime traces, warnings, and mirrored
logs.
Standard input may be claimed either by one
.B -
file operand or by
.BR io.stdin() ,
never both.
Batch mode never prompts and never saves a dirty buffer implicitly.
.PP
Commands requiring a screen or interactive answer raise a catchable
.B capability
error.
Use direct scripted alternatives: for example,
.B buf.open(path)
instead of the file picker,
.B io.glob(pattern)
instead of the file finder, and
.B ed.run(name, args)
instead of entering E mode.
.IR docs/batch.md
contains the complete stdio and refusal tables.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The script or requested inspection completed.
.TP
.B 1
Usage, compilation, or an uncaught ordinary runtime error.
.TP
.B 3
An unreadable file, host I/O error, or uncaught
.B capability
or
.B io
error.
.TP
.B 4
An internal invariant failed.
.PP
.B yew fl
never returns 2; that status belongs to a script failure under
.BR "yew \-\-batch" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B YEW_RUNTIME_DIR
Override the installed runtime directory used for file imports.
.TP
.B YEW_FL_GC_STRESS
When present, request a garbage collection at every safe allocation point.
This is a developer diagnostic and is intentionally slow.
.TP
.B FL_GC_STRESS
Compatibility spelling for
.BR YEW_FL_GC_STRESS .
.TP
.B YEW_FL_DUMP_BAD_CHUNK
Write malformed-bytecode diagnostics to the named path.
This is a developer diagnostic.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " HOME
Select the user Fletch import directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
Evaluate an expression:
.nf
.RS
yew fl \-e '1 + 2'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run a script from standard input:
.nf
.RS
printf 'import io\enio.print("hello")\en' | yew fl
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check a file without executing it:
.nf
.RS
yew fl \-c tools/check.fl
.RE
.fi
.PP
Inspect compiled functions:
.nf
.RS
yew fl \-\-dump-bytecode tools/check.fl
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run without host I/O authority:
.nf
.RS
yew fl \-\-caps none \-\-origin cli tools/check.fl
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR yew (1),
.BR yew-pkg (1)
